# EVERY THREE-POINT CLAIM IN EVERY DOCUMENT, WITH THE ELEMENT THAT MADE IT, THE WIDTH IT
# SPANS, AND THE TWO ANSWERS `Claim` REQUIRES OF IT. One row per claim: what would narrow
# this range, and who owns the edge it would have to move.
#
# ⭐⭐⭐ THE GRAIN IS THE CLAIM AND NOT THE LAYER, AND THAT IS THE WHOLE POINT OF THE FILE.
#    `Claim` is the most reused type in the schema: demands, nameplates, quanta, windows,
#    draws, slacks, shares, factors, coupling strengths and eliminated quantities are all
#    Claims. Every one of those lives in the table of the thing it describes, which is the
#    right shape for asking about a demand and the wrong shape for asking about a claim.
#    Twelve different parents in this corpus, across two schemas.
#
# ⭐⭐ AND IT CARRIES `layer`, WHICH IS WHAT LETS A CLAIM-GRAINED RELATION JOIN BACK. The
#    ancestor axis supplies it at ingest; NULL means the claim is about a RELATION between
#    layers -- a coupling strength, an elimination quantity, a part factor -- rather than
#    about one. Without it a rule needing both grains has to read the per-table copy and
#    reach one parent of twelve.
#
# ⛔⛔ AND IT IS THE HALF OF THE PAIR EASIEST TO LOSE. Keyed on a bare document ordinal with no
#    way back to the claim, `narrowsWhen` and `boundOrigin` can be COUNTED and never COMPARED
#    against the width they describe, and both rules that make that comparison then run over
#    `layer.demand_*` -- 40 claims of 182, and the only 40 with no point value among them. One
#    reports VACUOUS; the other passes on a corpus containing a violation it cannot reach.
#
# ⭐⭐ AND THE TWO COLUMNS ARE ONE INSTRUMENT. `narrowsWhen` says what would make the range
#    SMALLER; `boundOrigin` says who owns the edge it would have to move. Same width, and
#    three different courses of action depending on the answers: renegotiate, measure again,
#    or accept it. Read apart they are two censuses; read together they are advice.
-- pm:Claim, with its required pm:narrowsWhen and pm:boundOrigin, joined on the claim.
SELECT c.filing, c.seq, c.owns, c.layer,
       c.low, c.mode, c.high, c.unit,
       c.denominator, c.denominator_kind, c.denominator_absent,
       c.prov_party, c.prov_standing_taxonomy, c.prov_standing_value, c.prov_standing_absent,
       c.low = c.high AS is_a_point,
       n.condition    AS narrows_condition,
       n.kind         AS narrows_kind,
       n.absent       AS narrows_absent,
       b.origin,
       b.absent       AS origin_absent
FROM pm.claim c
JOIN pm.narrowing    n USING (filing, seq)
JOIN pm.bound_origin b USING (filing, seq)
